WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.633
AWS core services.

2
00:00:02.933 --> 00:00:10.467
Choosing between EC2, Lambda, and ECS is a cost, latency, and ops decision you should argue out loud.

3
00:00:11.667 --> 00:00:14.200
AWS compute comes in three shapes.

4
00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:16.642
EC2 is a raw virtual machine.

5
00:00:16.642 --> 00:00:21.000
Full control, always on, always billed, and yours to patch.

6
00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:22.683
Lambda is a function.

7
00:00:22.683 --> 00:00:29.200
It scales to zero, so idle costs nothing, but it caps at fifteen minutes and pays a cold-start tax.

8
00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:35.433
ECS runs containers with managed orchestration, and with Fargate, you never see a server.

9
00:00:36.633 --> 00:00:37.831
So how do you choose?

10
00:00:37.831 --> 00:00:38.800
Look at the load.

11
00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:43.246
Spiky and low volume, like ten requests a day, belongs on Lambda.

12
00:00:43.246 --> 00:00:44.267
You pay pennies.

13
00:00:44.567 --> 00:00:51.267
Steady and high volume belongs on containers or EC2, because per-invocation pricing gets brutal.

14
00:00:51.567 --> 00:00:57.000
Weird dependencies, GPUs, or long jobs go to EC2, the escape hatch.

15
00:00:58.200 --> 00:00:59.800
Here is the cost argument.

16
00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:06.000
At ten requests a day, Lambda is nearly free, while an idle EC2 box costs real money.

17
00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:11.733
At a hundred requests a second, Lambda cost climbs, and cold starts hurt tail latency.

18
00:01:12.033 --> 00:01:13.733
Containers win at steady scale.

19
00:01:14.933 --> 00:01:16.867
Here are the three shapes as commands.

20
00:01:17.167 --> 00:01:19.633
Lambda is a function invoke, paid per call.

21
00:01:19.933 --> 00:01:23.433
ECS scales by raising the desired container count.

22
00:01:23.733 --> 00:01:29.000
And with EC2, you simply log in to a computer that is yours, including the patching.

23
00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:32.367
Here is the story to tell in an interview.

24
00:01:32.667 --> 00:01:39.600
The FastAPI backend starts with about fifty requests a day, on Lambda behind API Gateway.

25
00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:42.877
Launch week brings forty requests a second.

26
00:01:42.877 --> 00:01:47.100
Cold starts push p99 past three seconds, and the bill climbs.

27
00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:51.133
So it moves to ECS Fargate with two always-warm tasks.

28
00:01:52.333 --> 00:01:53.192
Let's recap.

29
00:01:53.192 --> 00:01:56.267
EC2 is a virtual machine you own and patch.

30
00:01:56.567 --> 00:01:59.400
Lambda scales to zero, but has cold starts.

31
00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:02.067
ECS runs managed containers.

32
00:02:02.367 --> 00:02:04.443
Match the shape to the load.

33
00:02:04.443 --> 00:02:05.333
Next up: S3.
